                     </titlePage>
                     <extent xml:id="extent_822254ce">1 folio</extent>
                     <dimensions unit="cm" xml:id="dimensions_e7b1e92d">22.4x30.6</dimensions>
                     <physMedium xml:id="physMedium_6611dc75">In 2<rend xml:id="idm114" rend="sup">o</rend>; oblong format; music (fol. 1r–v); in all a single folio. Hand-ruled paper, 10 staves (10 mm, single rastrum used) in light brown ink and music in dark brown ink. Title, tempo marking, name of composer, clefs, braces and instructions (soli and tutti) in red ink which has faded and is now difficult to discern; '#6' has later been added to the work in ink at the top right-hand corner and is most likely a numbering added at a later stage. Transcript produced by a professional copyist. The indication of solo and tutti implies that the concerto was originally for harpsichord and ensemble or orchestra. The use of the G2-clef in the keyboard's left-hand stave implies that the copy most likely was produced during the second half of the eighteenth century. The part shows signs having having been used (marks of page turns). Paper of a fairly dense quality.</physMedium>
